Nearly every backend engineer has written or reviewed distributed locking code that looks like this:<br><br><pre><code>// Common anti-pattern: Naive time-based lease
const acquired = await redis.set('lock:invoice:101', workerId, 'NX', 'PX', 10000);
if (acquired) {
  const invoice = await storage.load(101);
  const updated = applyHeavyTaxCalculation(invoice);
  await storage.save(101, updated);
  await redis.eval(releaseLockScript, ['lock:invoice:101'], [workerId]);
}</code></pre><br>This pattern assumes two properties that real-world networks and operating systems do not provide: <strong>bounded execution time</strong> and <strong>perfect clock synchronization</strong>.<br><br>Consider what happens under realistic production conditions:<br>• <strong>The Zombie Pause</strong>: Worker 1 acquires the lock with a 10-second Time-To-Live (TTL). Right before writing to storage, Worker 1 encounters a 15-second Stop-The-World garbage collection pause, an OS memory page swap, or a virtual machine live migration.<br>• <strong>The Silent Expiration</strong>: While Worker 1 is frozen, its 10-second TTL elapses in Redis. The lock key is automatically evicted.<br>• <strong>Concurrent Acquisition</strong>: Worker 2 requests the lock, succeeds, and begins modifying invoice 101.<br>• <strong>Split-Brain Overwrite</strong>: Worker 1 finally wakes up. Process execution resumes right at <code>await storage.save(101, updated)</code>. Unaware that 15 seconds have passed and that its lease is gone, Worker 1 writes its data to disk, silently overwriting Worker 2's valid changes.<br><br>Background heartbeat threads (such as Redisson's lock watchdog) help prevent leases from expiring during long processing loops, but they cannot solve the pause problem: if the entire JVM or node freezes, the watchdog freezes with it.
